WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.760
OK, now here’s a question.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.320
What’s the biggest living organism
on the planet?

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.760
A whale? A redwood tree?

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.400
Arguably it’s armillaria,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.640
a huge honey fungus
living in a forest in Oregon, USA.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.120
It covers over
nine square kilometres

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.560
and is thought to be
at least 2,400 years old.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.520
Oh, and it glows in the dark. 

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.720
Mind blown?  

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.920
What if we also told you
that other types of fungi

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.640
can absorb oil spills,
control insects’ brains

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.360
and that fungi in general

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.280
are essential
to all terrestrial life on Earth? 

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.200
And there’s still so much
we don’t know -

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.160
more than 90%
of the fungi in the world

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.520
are currently unknown to science.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.040
You could say there’s
mush-room for learning. 

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.120
Now that we’ve got that terrible pun
out of the way,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.600
we can take a closer look

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.320
at some fascinating facts

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.840
that will make you a real fun-guy
at parties.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.480
Sorry.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.880
If you think of mushrooms
as vegetables on your pizza,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.240
you’re not alone,

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.920
thanks to the Victorian botanists
who classified fungi with plants.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.880
In fact, fungi are neither
plant nor animal.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.880
They are in a kingdom
of their own,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.560
but are genetically
much closer to animals.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.600
The genetic code
of a mushroom

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.080
has more in common with ours
than a potato’s.  

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.000
Maybe don’t mention that
to your vegan friends. 

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.760
Fungi also have the ability
to control other organisms.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.440
While some species of fungi
break down dead leaves and wood,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.080
like the helpful housemate

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.240
who always remembers
to take the bins out,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.120
other fungi are mycorrhizal,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.000
they plug into the
root tissue of plants

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.800
and pass precious
nutrients between them. 

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.160
This process forms a dense
fungal network called a mycelium.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.120
Soil often contains masses
of these mycelial threads

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.560
connecting different plants,
like fibre-optic cables.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.680
In woods, the network is known
as the wood-wide web

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.720
because plants use it to
communicate with one another.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.720
In a fertile pasture, the fungi
feeding on plants below ground

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.000
can be many times heavier than
the cows eating the grass above it.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.440
Some fungi get other species
to do the work for them. 

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:23.920
The Leucoagaricus uses chemical
signals to ‘employ’ leaf-cutter ants.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.200
The ants work together
to form a supply chain

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.360
that can deliver up to 60,000 leaf
fragments an hour to the fungus.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.960
In return, it produces miniscule
ant-sized mushrooms to feed them.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.040
Other fungi employ
more gruesome methods.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.360
Cordyceps infiltrate
their insect host -

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.760
for example ants or beetles -

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.760
and control their brains,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.640
forcing them to climb up high,

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.640
and then bursting out of their bodies
to scatter their spores to the wind. 

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:56.920
Talk about evil genius.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.720
Not content with insect mind-control,

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.160
some types of fungi
can trouble humans,

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.840
causing irritations
like athlete’s foot,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.000
as well as the more
deadly histoplasmosis. 

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.200
Ergot, a fungus that grows on wheat
and rye, can literally send you mad -

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.480
eating bread made from affected flour

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.640
is likely to have contributed to mass
hallucinations in medieval Europe.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.040
And you don’t have
to be a seasoned forager

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.680
to know that some species
of fungi are highly toxic. 

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:30.400
If I were you, I’d avoid anything
with an intimidating biker-gang name

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.920
like Autumn Skullcap
or Destroying Angels. 

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.600
While fungi can, in some cases,
be extremely dangerous,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.080
they are also
an invaluable resource.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.400
For a start, fungi produce
life-saving medicines

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.720
such as penicillin.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.080
Yeast is a fungus,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.880
without which we’d have
no bread, wine, beer or cheese.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.440
Even Quorn is made from a fungus.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.200
Basically, without fungi there would
be no middle-class dinner parties.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.960
Mycelium can be used
to make packaging and clothing. 

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.840
Fungi have been used
to clean up oil spills,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.840
and an itaconic acid
derived from a fungus

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.200
is even used to make Lego!

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:14.720
Fungi are also eco-warriors.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.240
They are absolutely essential
to a healthy soil,

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.960
and play a key role in trapping
CO2 fixed by plants into the soil,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.240
preventing its return
to the atmosphere.  

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.160
Over a thousand billion tonnes
of carbon

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.400
are locked in the world’s soils.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.520
That’s more than twice
the amount of carbon

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.040
held by all the trees on Earth.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.520
If the activity of soil fungi
were to increase,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.080
they could potentially solve
global warming on their own!

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.720
But despite their many uses,
and dangers,

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.480
of course fungi aren’t
actually ‘good’ or ‘evil’.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.320
They are an incredibly adaptable
and distinct life-form,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.400
playing a key role in
terrestrial life on the planet.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:54.560
And for that -

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.440
and not just because they
taste great on pizzas -

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.680
they should be celebrated. 

